WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:11.680
The promise of virtual reality, VR is indeed&nbsp;
truly incredible. If one day it comes to fruition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.360
doctors could be trained to perform surgery in a&nbsp;
virtual environment, we could train better pilots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.760
with better flight simulators, expose astronauts&nbsp;
to virtual zero-gravity simulations, you name it.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.840
An important part of doing many of these is&nbsp;
simulating walking in a virtual environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.840
You see, we can be located in a small room,&nbsp;
put on a VR headset and enter a wonderful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.840
expansive virtual world. However, as we&nbsp;
start walking, we immediately experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:49.280
a big problem. What is that problem?&nbsp;
Well, we bump into things. As a remedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.320
we could make our virtual world smaller,&nbsp;
but that would defeat the purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:00.240
This earlier technique addresses this&nbsp;
walking problem spectacularly by redirection.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.760
So, what is this redirection thing exactly?&nbsp;
Redirection is a simple concept that changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:12.080
our movement in the virtual world so it deviates&nbsp;
from our real path in the room in a way that both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:18.320
lets us explore the virtual world, and not bump&nbsp;
into walls and objects in reality in the meantime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.000
Here you can see how the blue and orange lines&nbsp;
deviate, which means that the algorithm is at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.840
work. With this, we can wander about in&nbsp;
a huge and majestic virtual landscape or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.600
a cramped bar, even when being confined&nbsp;
to a small physical room. Loving the idea.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.360
But there is more to interacting with&nbsp;
virtual worlds than walking, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.800
look at this tech demo that requires more&nbsp;
precise hand movements. How do we perform these?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.040
Well, the key is here. Controllers!&nbsp;
Clearly, they work, but can we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.560
rid of them? Can we just opt for a more&nbsp;
natural solution and use our hands instead?

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.160
Well, hold on to your papers, because this&nbsp;
new work uses a learning-based algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:10.320
to teach a head-mounted camera to tell the&nbsp;
orientation of our hands at all times. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.000
the quality of the execution matters a great&nbsp;
deal, so we have to ensure at least three things.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.240
One is that the hand tracking&nbsp;
happens with minimal latency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.160
which means that we see our actions&nbsp;
immediately, with minimal delay.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.520
Two, we need low jitter, which means that&nbsp;
the keypoints of the reconstructed hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.720
should not change too much from frame to frame.&nbsp;
This happens a great deal with previous methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.680
and what about the new one? Oh&nbsp;
yes, much smoother. Checkmark!

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.000
Note that the new method also remembers&nbsp;
the history of the hand movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.680
and therefore can deal with difficult occlusion&nbsp;
situations. For instance, look at the pinky here!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.960
A previous technique would not know what’s going&nbsp;
on with it, but, this new one knows exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.600
is going on because it has information&nbsp;
on what the hand was doing a moment ago.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.520
And three, this needs to work in all kinds&nbsp;
of lighting conditions. Let’s see if it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:13.520
reconstruct a range of mythical creatures in&nbsp;
poor lighting conditions. Yes, these ducks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:22.080
are reconstructed just as well as the mighty&nbsp;
pokemonster, and, these scissors too. Bravo!

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:28.640
So, what can we do with this? A great deal. For&nbsp;
instance, we can type on a virtual keyboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.680
or implement all kinds of virtual user&nbsp;
interfaces that we can interact with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.160
We can also organize imaginary boxes, and&nbsp;
of course, we can’t leave out the the Two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:46.240
Minute Papers favorite, going into a&nbsp;
physics simulation and playing with it.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.600
But of course, not everything&nbsp;
is perfect here, however. Look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.280
Hand-hand interactions don’t work so well, so&nbsp;
folks who prefer virtual reality applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.560
that include washing our hands should look&nbsp;
elsewhere. But of course, one step at a time.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.120
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

